<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Review problems on partial derivatives
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-partial-derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="chapter-partial-derivatives" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems compute higher order partial derivatives. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="chapter-partial-derivatives" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-pd-1">
<title>Higher order computation</title>
<statement>
<p>
Prove that <m>c(x,t) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-\frac{x^2}{4Dt}}</m>
is a solution of the diffusion equation
<md>
\frac{\partial c}{\partial t} = D\,\frac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We compute the needed derivatives:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial c}{\partial t}
\amp = -\frac{1}{2\sqrt{D}\,t^{3/2}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}
+ \frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,\frac{x^2}{4Dt^2}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial c}{\partial x}
\amp = -\frac{x}{2Dt}\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}
\amp = -\frac{1}{2Dt\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}
+ \frac{x^2}{4D^2t^2}\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
We see that the right hand side of the third equation is
<m>\dfrac{1}{D}</m> times the right hand side of the first, i.e.
multiplying <m>\dfrac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}</m> by <m>D</m>
gives exactly <m>\dfrac{\partial c}{\partial t}</m>, showing that
<m>c</m> satisfies the diffusion equation.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-28">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y,z) = xy + 3y^2z^3 + \ln(xz)</m>. Compute
<m>\dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}</m> and
<m>\dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y \partial z}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note first that <m>\ln(xz) = \ln x + \ln z</m>, which makes the
differentiation easier. Treating the other variables as constants,
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = y + \frac1x
\;\Rightarrow\;
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2} = -\frac{1}{x^2}.
</md>
For the mixed partial, differentiate first with respect to <m>z</m>
and then with respect to <m>y</m>:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z} = 9y^2z^2 + \frac1z
\;\Rightarrow\;
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y \partial z}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(9y^2z^2 + \frac1z\right)
= 18yz^2.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
